---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import MetaTagGenerator from '@/components/tools/generators/MetaTagGenerator.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="meta-tag-generator">
  <MetaTagGenerator client:load />

  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>How to use this</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Write the title and description first. Watch the pixel width, not the character count, because Google truncates by pixels.</li>
      <li>Add the canonical URL. It must be absolute, with the scheme, and point to the version of the page you want indexed.</li>
      <li>Do not change the robots switches without a reason. The default, <code>index, follow</code>, is correct for almost all pages.</li>
      <li>Fill in the share image and check the social preview. A missing or broken image is the most common reason a shared link looks bad.</li>
      <li>Copy the block into the <code>&lt;head&gt;</code> of the page.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2>Title and description length, properly</h2>
    <p>
      Guides give a character count, but Google measures pixels. A desktop title renders at about 20 px
      Arial and is clipped near 600 px. The snippet is about 14 px over two lines, near 920 px in total.
      A “W” is more than three times the width of an “i”, so two titles of the same length can fit very
      differently. This meta tag generator measures the real rendered width of your text as you type.
    </p>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Element</th>
            <th>Pixel budget</th>
            <th>Roughly</th>
            <th>Advice</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Title, desktop</td><td>~600 px</td><td>50–60 characters</td><td>Put the keyword first and the brand at the end, after a pipe or dash</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Title, mobile</td><td>~500 px</td><td>45–50 characters</td><td>The most important words must be in the first 45</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Description</td><td>~920 px over two lines</td><td>120–158 characters</td><td>Write it as ad copy. It affects the click, not the ranking</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      Google rewrites the description about a third of the time, when other text on the page matches
      the query better than your summary. That is normal, and you do not need to fight it. A good
      description still controls the snippet for brand searches and exact-match queries, which convert
      best.
    </p>

    <h2>The tags that matter, and the ones that do not</h2>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Tag</th>
            <th>Effect</th>
            <th>Worth it?</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><code>&lt;title&gt;</code></td><td>Primary ranking factor and the clickable line in results</td><td>Essential</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>meta description</code></td><td>Controls the snippet. No direct ranking effect</td><td>Essential for click-through</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>link rel="canonical"</code></td><td>Consolidates duplicates onto one URL</td><td>Essential on any site with parameters or pagination</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>meta robots</code></td><td>Directive controlling indexing and link following</td><td>Only when you need something other than the default</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>og:*</code></td><td>Card layout on Facebook, LinkedIn, Slack, Discord, WhatsApp</td><td>Essential if links get shared</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>twitter:*</code></td><td>X card type and attribution</td><td>Useful. Without it, X uses Open Graph</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>meta keywords</code></td><td>None. Ignored by Google since 2009, a spam signal to Bing</td><td>No. This tool leaves it out</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>meta author</code>, <code>meta revisit-after</code></td><td>No search effect</td><td>Harmless, but not useful</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h2>Robots directives, and how to lose all your traffic</h2>
    <p>
      <code>noindex</code> is a directive, not a hint. Put it in a live template and all pages that use
      that template leave the index within days, and take weeks to come back. Two rules:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>Never combine <code>noindex</code> with a <code>Disallow</code> in robots.txt. If the crawler cannot fetch the page, it never sees the tag, so the URL stays in the index as a bare result with no description. Allow the crawl and serve the noindex.</li>
      <li><code>nofollow</code> at page level is almost never correct. It stops link equity from flowing to your own pages. To mark individual links, use <code>rel="nofollow"</code>, <code>rel="ugc"</code> or <code>rel="sponsored"</code> on the anchor.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Other useful values, which combine with commas: <code>noarchive</code> removes the cached copy,
      <code>nosnippet</code> removes the description, <code>max-snippet:150</code> limits its length,
      <code>noimageindex</code> keeps your images out of image search, and
      <code>unavailable_after:2026-12-31T23:59:59Z</code> schedules removal.
    </p>

    <h2>Canonical URLs</h2>
    <p>
      A canonical tag tells search engines which URL is the real one when several show the same
      content. A wrong canonical can remove a page from the index as surely as <code>noindex</code>.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>Always absolute: <code>https://example.com/page</code>, never <code>/page</code>.</li>
      <li>Self-referential on each page. A page whose canonical points at itself is unambiguous.</li>
      <li>Match the served version exactly. Trailing slash, protocol, subdomain and letter case all count.</li>
      <li>Do not canonicalise paginated pages to page one. Each page of a listing has different content and should stand alone.</li>
      <li>Never point a canonical at a page that redirects, or at a page that is itself canonicalised elsewhere.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Share images</h2>
    <p>
      Use 1200 × 630 pixels, under 5 MB, PNG or JPEG, served over HTTPS from an absolute URL. Most
      crawlers ignore relative paths and images behind authentication. Add <code>og:image:alt</code>.
      Several platforms show it, and it is the only accessible description a shared card gets.
    </p>
    <p>
      If a card still looks wrong after you fix the tags, the platform shows a cached scrape.
      Facebook’s Sharing Debugger, LinkedIn’s Post Inspector and X’s card validator each have a
      “scrape again” action that refreshes it.
    </p>

    <h2>Nothing here touches a server</h2>
    <p>
      The previews render from what you type, and the pixel measurements come from a canvas in this
      tab. The only network request is the browser loading the share image you entered, so you can see
      if the URL works. Your unpublished titles, descriptions and staging URLs stay on your machine.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
